# FacilityOps Backend environment (copy this file to backend/.env)
#
# backend/app/config.py loads this file. The only required variable is
# DATABASE_URL. If it is unset, the backend falls back to a local SQLite
# database at backend/data/facilityops.db.

# --- Database (required for full functionality) ---
# PostgreSQL connection string. Recommended: a free Neon PostgreSQL database
# (https://neon.tech) — both local dev and the deployed Render service can share
# one instance.
#
# Format:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxxx.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
#
# The backend automatically normalizes `postgresql://` to the psycopg3 dialect,
# so no extra driver config is needed.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host/database

# --- Groq (optional, enables live LLM answers in the Facility Copilot) ---
# Get a free key at https://console.groq.com (Models → API Keys). Set the same
# value in the Render service's env var dashboard for production.
#
# Without this key the Copilot still works — it falls back to a deterministic
# keyword-based composer over the same live agent data. With it, /api/copilot/chat
# answers via the Groq LLM (llama-3.3-70b-versatile) grounded in real agent
# factoids; if a Groq call ever fails it falls back automatically.
#
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Notes ---
# * The Neon URL is a secret — it must only ever live in this file (gitignored)
#   and in the Render service's env var dashboard. Never commit it.
# * The GROQ_API_KEY is a secret too — same rule: backend/.env (gitignored) +
#   Render env vars only. Never commit it.
# * The database is seeded automatically on first start (see docs/WORKFLOW.md).
# * If you leave DATABASE_URL unset, the app runs on local SQLite — usable for
#   a quick smoke test, but some agents use PostgreSQL-only SQL functions.
